   "format": "advanced",
   "processed": "<h1>UCSF Adult Outpatient Susceptibility Data: <em>E. coli</em> Urinary Isolates<sup>1</sup></h1><p><strong>AMP/SUL</strong>-ampicillin-sulbactam, <strong>CIP</strong>-ciprofloxacin, <strong>CTRX</strong>-ceftriaxone,&nbsp;<strong>CZLN</strong>-cefazolin, <strong>NTF</strong>-nitrofurantoin,&nbsp;<strong>T/S</strong>-trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole<br><br>Reported as % susceptible, tested from urinary sites</p><table class=\"table\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"888\"><thead><tr height=\"98\"><th height=\"98\" scope=\"col\" width=\"262\"><strong>Organism&nbsp;</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"100\"><strong>Total Isolates&nbsp;</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"107\"><strong>AMP/SUL<sup>2</sup></strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"84\"><strong>CIP</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"84\"><strong>CTRX</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"84\"><strong>CZLN<sup>3</sup>&nbsp;</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"84\"><strong>NTF</strong></th><th style=\"text-align:center;\" scope=\"col\" width=\"84\"><strong>T/S&nbsp;</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr height=\"64\"><td height=\"64\" width=\"262\"><em>Escherichia coli</em>, urine</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"100\">554</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"107\">65</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"84\">85</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"84\">86</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"84\">85</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"84\">98</td><td style=\"text-align:center;\" width=\"84\">70</td></tr></tbody></table><p><sup>1</sup>Adult outpatient susceptibilities include data from 1/1/2025 - 12/31/2025; <sup>2</sup>Surrogate test to predict oral amoxicillin/clavulanate; <sup>3</sup>Surrogate test to predict oral agents cefdinir, cefpodoxime, cefprozil, cefuroxime, and cephalexin when used for&nbsp;uncomplicated UTIs</p>",
